## Proof

Recall that the Jacobson radical $\text{rad}(S)$ equals the intersection of all maximal right ideals of $S$. Since $J$ is a maximal right ideal, we have
$$\text{rad}(S) \subseteq J.$$
Given $z \in \text{rad}(S)$, it follows that $z \in J$.

Now consider the right ideal $sS + J$. Since $J$ is a **maximal** right ideal and $sS + J$ is a right ideal containing $J$ (as $J \subseteq sS + J$), maximality forces
$$sS + J = J \quad\text{or}\quad sS + J = S.$$

We rule out the first possibility. Suppose for contradiction that $sS + J = J$. Then $sS \subseteq J$, and in particular $s = s \cdot 1 \in sS \subseteq J$, so $s \in J$.

Since $J$ is a **right ideal**, it is closed under right multiplication by any element of $S$: $J \cdot S \subseteq J$. With $s \in J$ and $z \in S$, this gives
$$sz \in J.$$

But this contradicts the hypothesis $sz \notin J$.

Therefore $sS + J \neq J$, and by maximality of $J$ we conclude
$$\boxed{S = sS + J}.$$

